    "narrative": "Today\u2019s lineup is anchored by **Taylor Ward** (71.46) and **Jonathan Aranda** (70.47), both of whom benefit from favorable matchups against pitchers with xERAs north of 4.60. **Taylor Ward** is our strongest play, buoyed by an elite 16% park factor and an advantageous left-handed platoon split against Schultz. Similarly, **Jonathan Aranda** maximizes our scoring potential today with a significant 15% boost from his SP skill matchup and a +5% wind-out factor that should carry his flyballs out of the park. We are also leaning on **Daulton Varsho** (67.28) and **Jung Hoo Lee** (65.65) specifically for their SB upside; both are facing pitchers with slow deliveries, providing a clear path to production in our scarcest category.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the card was keeping **Lawrence Butler** (41.52) on the bench. While his park factor is enticing, his matchup against Dreyer (2.96 xERA) forced the optimizer to fade him in favor of the more stable efficiency provided by **Masyn Winn** and **Geraldo Perdomo**. We must monitor the risk surrounding **Bryson Stott** (52.29); while he holds a positive platoon edge, his encounter with Skenes carries an inherent \"elite arm\" penalty (-19% SP Skill) that limits his ceiling. Elsewhere, we remain in a holding pattern regarding our roster depth, as the injuries to **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** and **Will Smith** continue to compress our daily scoring floor, forcing us to lean on high-variance bench depth like **Matt Chapman**, **Ryan Waldschmidt**, **Dylan Beavers**, **Gleyber Torres**, **Brendan Donovan**, **Jeremiah Jackson**, and **Drew Gilbert** to fill gaps where necessary. **Cal Raleigh** remains unavailable due to a scheduled off-day, meaning we must rely on **Yainer Diaz** (48.86) to manage the backstop position despite a lower projected output against Bradley.",
